breaking news now and uk health  officials say everyone over the age of  50 as well as health workers and those  most at risk of complications from cobit  19 will be offered booster jabs in the  autumn it comes as cases of coronavirus  continue to surge across the uk with an  increase of almost a third in the past  week joining me now is professor adam  finn member of the joint committee on  vaccination and immunization uh thanks  so much for joining me professor finn so  uh  the recommendation uh as i think i'm  writing saying uh a couple of months ago  was for those over 65 to get the booster  shot clearly the government's enacting  those over 50. talk us through why uh  suddenly we're increasing the number of  people that that need to get this  booster  uh yes good afternoon wilfred uh the  there's always been clear that we were  going to need a booster program and that  age was going to be the  critical factor  in determining  uh exactly who should be targeted for  these vaccines but it wasn't entirely  clear  earlier in the  in the summer  where the line should be drawn  but i think the evidence we've now seen  makes it clear in terms of what we're  seeing at the moment that we should go  for a very straightforward simple and uh  clear program of offering both influenza  and cobit boosters to everyone over the  age of 50 as well as people who for  other reasons are at high risk of  serious illness because what these  vaccines can do is to keep you out of  hospital  keep the nhs going  and minimize the chance of disruption  when we get to the depths of the winter  and and again just talk us through why  it's so crucial to do  uh as we approach winter as opposed to  as you know you're saying already these  cases have been shooting up of of late  why will it get worse still in the  winter  yeah it's always hard to predict the  future and i guess we while we knew that  we might be seeing a wave at this time  we certainly had no  strong expectation of that happening  this virus continues to catch us out and  do the unexpected but if you look back  over the last two winters we have seen  uh significant waves in the winter time  and indeed we do see that every year in  normal times with all of the other  respiratory viruses that that circulate  around so particularly december january  february  sometimes into march  very difficult periods for the nhs every  year with lots of people particularly  old people being admitted to hospital  with respiratory infections so uh  although we can't guarantee it uh it's  certainly a serious risk that this will  happen again this winter and we want to  protect against that happening as best  we can and just to clarify so the the  flu the influenza vaccine is is that the  same recommendation only those over 50  exactly right so in in the past flu  vaccines have been offered to the over  65's again it's a an illness that is  particularly a problem for uh for the  elderly um last year we extended that  down to the age of 50 um and we're doing  exactly the same thing this year um we  really do need to protect the health  service as best we can and the the risks  of serious illness do go up with age  certainly by the time you get to my age  in the early 60s you are an at-risk  person so people like me and our my age  group definitely need to be uh immunized  as well  in terms of why we're only going for the  over 50s and not everyone again or all  adults again is that because of  a good reason because younger people  don't need it and then the variants now  aren't too threatening or a bad reason  because we don't have the supply or we  don't have perhaps the ability to  distribute it quickly enough  no it is a good reason  we are seeing very little serious  illness now in healthy younger people  both adults and children  and i think that's a combination of the  vaccinations they've many of them have  received as well as the fact of course  that they've  most people have now actually had  coveted at least once uh in addition to  being vaccinated so there's a lot of  immunity out there in the population  and we're not seeing significant  problems with serious illness now these  vaccines are not very reliable at  preventing infection as many of us have  discovered in recent weeks and months  but they do reliably protect against  serious illness so we need to target  them at the people who are at risk of  that serious illness and that is the  older people not the younger ones and  just finally uh professor whilst we've  got you here a comment on the latest  numbers i think we saw that go up by a  third uh this week again is this a sort  of case of they're worrying we've got to  keep an eye on it but given that  hospitalizations and deaths aren't  following suit to the same extent that  that we don't need to be too worried  well i think it is worrying i mean it's  an enormous number of infections and  it's certainly being very disruptive a  lot of people in my clinical research  team and laboratory are off sick with  kovid at the moment and i'm sure  everyone who's listening to this is  being affected by one way or another  so it's not a trivial problem by any  manner of means uh and in addition as  you uh just heard in your previous  report we're seeing significant  pressures on the health service right  now which is very uncharacteristic for  this time of year so i think people  should recognize that this is not  trivial  it does need  to be thought about and any efforts that  people can make to try and reduce the  transmission of infection  will help bring that peak down professor  finn thanks so much for joining me much  appreciated
 

